# O-0009 — deliberate-provisional-naming-as-method

## Statement

Using intentionally informal, goofy, or placeholder terminology for a new
concept may be a deliberate method for letting underlying behavior emerge
before the concept is prematurely formalized.

This is not a claim that early or formal naming is wrong. It is an open
observation: provisional naming keeps a concept revisable, and this may
be valuable precisely because it defers judgment rather than making one.

## Working Model

A provisional name and a formal name may refer to the same underlying
thing. Naming does not change the referent; it changes the confidence
attached to it.

\[
x_{\text{provisional}} = x_{\text{formal}}
\]

\[
\text{weight}_t(x) \rightarrow \text{weight}_{t+1}(x), \quad
\text{weight}_{t+1} > \text{weight}_t
\]

Promotion from placeholder to formal name is not a transformation of
\(x\). It is a change in accumulated weight behind \(x\), with \(x\)'s
identity held fixed throughout.

## Candidate Source of Weight

\(\text{weight}\) is not currently a defined term in the system. It may
not need to be a new primitive — it may already be expressible as
accumulated provenance depth and independent witness count:

\[
\text{weight}(x) \approx f(\text{parentRefs depth of } x,\ \text{witness count of } x)
\]

This is a candidate reduction, not an established one. Whether existing
provenance and witness mechanisms already capture everything intended by
"weight" is an open question, not a conclusion.

## Context

Noted while reflecting on repeated use of informal or placeholder
language throughout this project's observations. The deliberate
informality is a known personal pattern: naming loosely as a check
against a tendency toward overcommitting to an idea before it has been
vetted — a practice applied to working with the system, not a claim
about the system itself.

## A Related Practice: Real-World Capture Moments

Moments away from active work are being used as deliberate triggers to
notice and flag ideas before they are lost, distinct from moments of
focused formal design work. Informal, out-of-context noticing appears to
be a real and recurring source of candidate material, prerequisite to
formal design sessions rather than a replacement for them.

## Governing Constraint

The system exists to hold the unknown as unknown.

Any convention — including this one — that quietly narrows what counts as
valid, nameable, or worth tracking has reduced the scope of the MAW to
only what is already known, which defeats its purpose.

This observation does not establish a threshold for when a placeholder
must become formal. That threshold is not defined here and may not be
definable in general.

## Minor Tenet (Unresolved)

A recurring impression has surfaced, informally, that some high-level
patterns in this project tend to resolve into a triadic shape ("a rule of
three") that has not yet been identified or named.

This is recorded as a hunch, not a rule. It has not been tested against
any concrete case. It should not be treated as evidence of a real pattern
until a specific instance can be named and checked, in the same manner as
any other candidate observation.

## Constraints

No claim is made that all placeholder terminology should remain
placeholder indefinitely.

No threshold for promotion from placeholder to formal concept is
established.

`weight` is not established as a new primitive; it is offered as a
possible reduction to existing provenance/witness concepts, pending test.

The "rule of three" impression is not claimed to be real. It is preserved
only as an unresolved hunch.

No new primitive or lifecycle stage proposed.

## Questions

- Does layer depth plus witness count already fully capture what "weight"
  was meant to describe, or is something left over?
- What (if anything) should determine when a placeholder is promoted to a
  concrete, named concept with its own timeline?
- Is the "rule of three" impression a real recurring structure, or an
  artifact of how humans (and language models) tend to pattern-match onto
  triads regardless of whether one exists?

